Process Creation and Process Synchronization using fork(), wait() and waitpid()

To understand process creation using fork() and process synchronization using wait() and waitpid() system calls.

## Program 1
This program creates three child processes using fork().
The parent process uses wait() to wait for any child process to complete.
It uses waitpid() to wait for a specific child process.
Finally, the parent waits for all child processes to complete.

## Program 2
This program demonstrates the creation of a parent and child process using fork().
The child process prints its process ID and terminates using exit().
The parent process prints its process ID, waits for 30 seconds, and then completes.

## System Calls Used
- fork() – Creates a new child process.
- getpid() – Returns the process ID.
- wait() – Waits for a child process to complete.
- waitpid() – Waits for a specific child process.
- sleep() – Suspends the process for a specified time.
- exit() – Terminates the process.

## Compilation
gcc practical4.c -o practical4

## Execution
./practical4

## Conclusion
The programs demonstrate process creation, parent-child relationships, and process synchronization using fork(), wait(), and waitpid().
